   250	## Optimization ladder
   251	
   252	Climb in this order and stop at the first rung that meets the budget.
   253	
   254	1. **Measure.** `py-spy record` for Python stacks, `perf` for native code,
   255	   `hyperfine` for whole commands. A long run writes its full log to a file.
   256	2. **Vectorize.** Broadcasting, batched `@` and stacked `np.linalg` replace
   257	   Python loops over elements; preallocate and keep dtypes concrete.
   258	3. **Batch replicates.** A null calibration or bootstrap runs across
   259	   replicates as arrays, with a gate proving the batched path returns the
   260	   scalar reference's bits ([vectorized-fits.md](runbooks/vectorized-fits.md)).
   261	4. **Pool independent items.** `parallel.ordered_map(function, items)` with a
   262	   module-level `function` and picklable items. Items that share a seeded
   263	   generator draw every input first, in the generator's order, and then map,
   264	   so pooled and serial runs write the same bytes. The pool spawns, so an
   265	   entry script calls it only under `if __name__ == "__main__":` and runs
   266	   from a file rather than stdin. The pool sets each unset BLAS and OpenMP
   267	   thread variable to one for its workers and keeps a value the caller
   268	   exported, which can oversubscribe native threads.
   269	5. **Move exact work to FLINT.** `scripts/exact_rank.py` gives
   270	   `rank_mod` (FLINT `nmod_mat`, a rank modulo a word-size prime, which
   271	   bounds the rational rank from below) and `rational_kernel` (FLINT
   272	   `fmpq_mat` reduced row echelon form, a rational null space). A finite-field
   273	   null space certifies nothing over the rationals; the certifying caller,
   274	   such as `d_locus_nullity.nullity`, checks each rational kernel vector in
   275	   Python integers.
   276	6. **Use an accelerator only where a run qualified it.** The host's CUDA Aer
   277	   build and Kaggle GPUs serve the circuits their notes measured; small
   278	   dynamic circuits stay on CPU ([substrates.md](notes/kaggle/substrates.md)).
   279	7. **Shard.** A pooled, batched run that still exceeds one host's budget
   280	   shards to Kaggle CPU sessions by item range, once the user has approved
   281	   the written case every simulator job and notebook session needs
   282	   ([AGENTS.md](../AGENTS.md) "Hardware services"). The successor null also
   283	   shards to the public repository's hosted runners
   284	   ([null-shards.md](runbooks/null-shards.md)).
   285	
   286	
   287	## Emulating another CPU with Intel SDE
   288	
   289	numpy's dispatched loops, OpenBLAS's `DYNAMIC_ARCH` kernel choice and glibc's
   290	libm ifuncs all pick their code path from CPUID, so one float64 computation can
   291	round differently on an AVX-512 runner than on the build host, an AMD Ryzen 5
   292	5600X3D (Zen 3, AVX2, no AVX-512). The Intel Software Development Emulator
   293	runs an unmodified process under a chosen Intel CPU's CPUID and instruction
   294	set, AVX-512 included, so the host reproduces an AVX-512 machine's natural code
   295	path without capping the dispatch (`OPENBLAS_CORETYPE`,
   296	`NPY_DISABLE_CPU_FEATURES`; [ci/AGENTS.md](../ci/AGENTS.md)).
   297	
   298	The host carries `intel-sde` 10.13.1 (chaotic-aur), installed at
   299	`/opt/intel-sde/sde64`. A run names the chip and passes the command after `--`:
   300	
   301	```sh
   302	/opt/intel-sde/sde64 -icx -- "$PYTHON" -B scripts/n21_dose_decisions.py RUN_DIR --out OUT.json
   303	```
   304	
   305	- `-icx` is an Ice Lake server and `-skx` a Skylake server; `-clx`, `-cpx`,
   306	  `-spr` and `-gnr` name other AVX-512 server chips, `-hsw` an AVX2-only
   307	  Haswell, and `/opt/intel-sde/sde64 -help` lists every chip. Emulate the
   308	  model the runner reports: `ci/run_gates.py` prints a `numeric stack:` line
   309	  with the CPU model, numpy's enabled dispatch targets and each loaded
   310	  OpenBLAS core before the first gate. SDE emulates Intel chips only, so an
   311	  AMD runner has no emulated counterpart.
   312	- The process sees the emulated CPUID, so numpy reports `AVX512F` and
   313	  `X86_V4` and dispatches its AVX-512 loops, and OpenBLAS selects its kernel
   314	  for that chip. Exported caps still apply, so one emulated run can take the
   315	  capped path and another the uncapped one.
   316	- The emulator executes each instruction with the hardware's IEEE semantics,
   317	  so the bits match a real chip of that generation wherever the result
   318	  depends only on the instruction path. Cache sizes, core counts and timing
   319	  are the host's, so a library that sizes its blocking from the cache or
   320	  threads by core count can still diverge from real hardware. Pin
   321	  `OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1` and compare against a real run before treating an
   322	  emulated replay as that chip's result.
   323	- Cost: importing numpy and threadpoolctl took 12.8 s, and the n21 dose
   324	  decisions run (about 5 s native) took 147 s, about 30 times native.
   325	
   326	Measured on the n21 dose-decisions record
   327	`results/live/2026-10-27T014354.701394Z/dose-decisions.json` (written in the
   328	runner image, `scripts/n21_dose_decisions.py`, whose replica covariance is
   329	`psi.T @ psi`, an OpenBLAS product):
   330	
   331	| Emulated chip | `OPENBLAS_CORETYPE=Haswell` | numpy cap | Fields that differ from the record |
   332	|---|---|---|---|
   333	| `-icx` | set | set | 0 |
   334	| `-icx` | set | unset | 0 |
   335	| `-icx` | unset | set | 85 |
   336	| `-icx` | unset | unset | 85 |
   337	| `-skx` | unset | unset | 85 |
   338	
   339	OpenBLAS's AVX-512 kernel alone moves the record under emulation, and
   340	numpy's dispatch leaves it unchanged. On the hosted runners of audit run
   341	40824932629, every shard reports numpy dispatching `X86_V3` and both
   342	OpenBLAS copies on the `Haswell` core, so both pins apply. The Intel Xeon
   343	Platinum 8573C shard (AVX-512) replays the record exactly. The AMD EPYC 9V74
   344	and 9V45 shards (Zen 4 and Zen 5, AVX-512) differ in 95 fields. Their
   345	`.arms.n21_orbit_window_dose_d7000_feedback_contrast.sigma` equals the
   346	emulated value of the unpinned Intel AVX-512 kernel, and their d10500 arm's
   347	sigma moves in no emulated run. So on those AMD CPUs a CPU-dependent path
   348	outside the reported numpy dispatch and OpenBLAS core still changes the
   349	rounding, and SDE cannot reproduce it. A record that depends on BLAS rounding
   350	replays on the host under emulation only after a real-hardware comparison
   351	has shown the two agree.
   352	
   353	## Correctness rules
   354	
   355	- Seed every generator and record the seed. Byte replay also needs the
   356	  pinned generator algorithm and dependency versions and a gate that
   357	  compares bytes: NumPy's seeded `Generator.binomial` changed between
   358	  releases (`scripts/atlas_fringe_amplitudes.py`).
   359	- float64 suffices wherever sampling sets the error. An exact claim uses
   360	  exact arithmetic, and extended precision only refines a float estimate.
   361	  A ball enclosure that excludes zero proves a minor nonzero and so bounds a
   362	  rank from below; no precision proves a value zero, so rank deficiency and
   363	  membership in a rank locus need exact arithmetic.
   364	- Float tolerances decide nothing about rank, nullity or local dimension.
   365	  A floating projection stops once a residual falls under its tolerance, so
   366	  a direction along which the residual vanishes to high order passes
   367	  uncorrected; such a test needs a scale where that order exceeds the
   368	  tolerance, or extended precision.
   369	- Run ruff and mypy through `ci/pinned_tool.py` on every touched Python file, and mypy over gate core-039's scope when a change alters a signature another module imports.
   370	- Record the versions a result depends on inside the result.
   371	- Mock outcomes (`--native-dry-run`) never enter an analysis or a figure.
     1	# Compute flags: memory caps, threads and solver options
     2	
     3	This runbook is the flag review that precedes every command whose cost or
     4	correctness depends on its options: computer algebra (Singular, msolve,
     5	Macaulay2), simulators (Aer, cuStateVec), Python pools, compilers, CI tools,
     6	cloud launchers and resource limiters, on the build host, Azure VMs, Kaggle
     7	kernels or any other substrate. The tool sections below cover the tools
     8	measured so far; a tool without a section gets one the first time it runs long. An agent or operator fills in the
     9	checklist at the end before the run starts and records the filled checklist
    10	beside the run's log. A run launched without it gets stopped and relaunched.
    11	
    12	[library-flags.md](library-flags.md) is the per-library companion: the environment
    13	variables, API options and build facts of every package the repository imports.
    14	
    15	## Why the review exists
    16	
    17	Every msolve run on the A_5 Rees-fiber eliminations died with exit 139 under a
    18	`prlimit --as` cap while its resident set sat far below that cap:
    19	
    20	| Cap | msolve flags | Death | Peak resident set |
    21	|---|---|---|---|
    22	| `prlimit --as` 80 GiB | `-t 4 -e 1 -g 2` | degree 7, "Allocating memory for hash-column lookup table failed" | 33.6 GB |
    23	| `prlimit --as` 28 GiB | `-t 3 -m 200000 -s 24 -l 1 -e 1 -g 2` | degree 6, "Enlarging exponent vector for hash table failed for esz = 268435456" | 28.0 GB |
    24	| `prlimit --as` 28 GiB | `-t 3 -s 24 -u 2 -e 1 -g 2` | same point, same message | 18.1 GB |
    25	
    26	`RLIMIT_AS`, which `prlimit --as` and Python's
    27	`resource.setrlimit(RLIMIT_AS, ...)` set, limits reserved virtual address
    28	space, not resident memory. The msolve 0.10.1 source names the two failing
    29	allocations: `enlarge_hash_table` in `src/neogb/hash.c` reallocates the whole
    30	exponent block at each doubling (`esz * evl * sizeof(exp_t)` bytes, with the
    31	old block still mapped until `realloc` returns), and `src/neogb/convert.c`
    32	reallocates the hash-to-column map (`(esld - 1) * sizeof(hi_t)` bytes) once per
    33	matrix. glibc also reserves one malloc arena per OpenMP thread. The address
    34	space therefore runs far ahead of the resident set, and the cap refuses an
    35	allocation that physical memory could serve, so the failure looked like a
    36	fixed internal limit. Several debugging rounds, including a consultation of
    37	five frontier models, went into msolve internals before reading the cap
    38	itself. The confirming run under a cgroup memory cap with the address-space
    39	limit lifted is recorded in the A_5 attachment evidence once it ends; until
    40	then the address-space explanation is the leading one, not a measured fact.
    41	
    42	The general rule: a resource limit, a default thread count, or an unexamined
    43	solver default can masquerade as a property of the mathematics. Read every
    44	flag of the launch line before blaming the algorithm.
    45	
    46	## Memory caps
    47	
    48	Every mechanism below is available; the review picks one memory limit and one
    49	time limit per run from what the substrate supports and records why.
    50	
    51	| Mechanism | What it limits | When it fits | Caveat |
    52	|---|---|---|---|
    53	| cgroup v2 `MemoryMax` (`systemd-run --user --scope -p MemoryMax=NG -p MemorySwapMax=0 CMD`) | resident memory plus page cache charged to the job; the kernel OOM-kills the job alone at the limit | default on the build host and Azure VMs | needs the `memory` controller delegated to the user manager (`cat /sys/fs/cgroup/user.slice/user-$(id -u).slice/user@$(id -u).service/cgroup.controllers`) and lingering (`loginctl enable-linger USER`) so the scope survives logout |
    54	| cgroup v2 `MemoryHigh` (same command, `-p MemoryHigh=NG`) | soft limit: the kernel reclaims and throttles above it instead of killing | pairing with `MemoryMax` to slow a job before it dies | throttling can stretch wall time severalfold |
    55	| container limit (`docker run --memory`, Kaggle's own 32 GB) | the container's cgroup | runs that already sit in a container | the whole container dies, every job in it included |
    56	| RSS watchdog (sample `VmRSS` of the job and its children, kill above N) | resident memory, at sample granularity | Kaggle kernels and other containers without a delegated cgroup; the multi-job template already samples every 30 s | a fast spike between samples escapes it |
    57	| `prlimit --as=BYTES CMD`, `ulimit -v`, `resource.setrlimit(RLIMIT_AS, ...)` | virtual address space reserved by the process | single-threaded programs whose virtual-to-resident ratio was measured small | refuses allocations far below physical memory for msolve, OpenMP programs, JIT runtimes and anything that reserves before touching |
    58	| `prlimit --rss`, `RLIMIT_RSS` | nothing on Linux since 2.6 | never | accepted and ignored by the kernel |
    59	| `prlimit --data`, `RLIMIT_DATA` | heap and private anonymous mappings | rarely; counts reservations like `--as` | same caveat as `--as` |
    60	| `timeout -k 10 SECONDS CMD` | wall time | every run, beside one memory limit | |
    61	| `systemd-run -p RuntimeMaxSec=` | wall time of the scope or service | runs already under `systemd-run` | |
    62	
