picture-codex.md:80:   That arithmetic is a hypothesis generator, not a computed full Hilbert series. Search for a small determinantal or scroll presentation and verify ideal equality. A \(2\times7\) matrix has 21 two-by-two minors, matching the quadratic-relation count; failure to find and verify such a presentation ends that particular hypothesis. The input measurements are the recorded VB quadratic model. [`decision-record.md:157–159`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:157)
picture-codex.md:82:3. **An independently bounded full Hilbert function.** If a proven relation-type bound limits every minimal fiber relation to degree \(D\), method A through \(D\) closes the algebra. Alternatively, a surjection from a candidate algebra to \(F(I)\), together with an independently established equal Hilbert series, proves equality degree by degree. Regularity of the candidate \(k[y]/K_2\) supplies neither condition; that shortcut was correctly rejected. [`decision-record.md:24`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:24)
picture-codex.md:84:A Cohen–Macaulay or regular-sequence argument must prove something about the relevant presentation. The generator counts already exceed the germ dimensions, so the supplied ideals \(I\) cannot themselves be regular sequences. A useful alternative is an unmixed candidate algebra plus independently established equal dimension and multiplicity: a nonzero kernel would then contribute top-dimensional multiplicity.
